=== verdict ===
measured: redis replace <=1s (apply 0.79s + start 0.86s, measured wall time until redis answers);
postgres replace <=1s (apply 0.76s + start 0.84s, until pg_isready); votes lost 50 of 50 across the redis replace;
vote/worker reconnected and the worker drained a post-replace vote.
decision-overlay: docs/decisions/0007-maxmemory-mutable-despite-replace-cost.md
human decision (2026-09-28): an infrastructure change made through the Deployment annotation is an
explicit approval for any downtime it causes, so downtime is accepted. The replaces are <=1s and
vote/worker reconnect, so the update cost is acceptable; redis maxmemory stays mutable and U1 stays
in scope. This replaces the spike's own 'refused' reading of criterion 1.
verdict provenance: the VERDICT line below is the recorded human decision, not a result the probe
measured. The probe measured the replace timings, the reconnect behaviour and the 50-of-50 vote loss;
it did not decide mutability. The decision is ADR 0007 - a review judges that ADR (its reasoning and
its scope), not the probe's numbers.
VERDICT: maxmemory mutable

=== side finding (not this step's checkpoint) ===
every re-apply of the stock redis/postgres module plans a replacement even with unchanged params:
ports { external = 0 } records the random host port in state and the next plan diffs it
(external = <assigned> -> 0 # forces replacement). S27's databases-only update (U2: container ID
unchanged) needs this addressed in the module, or the container is replaced on every apply.
done
